  {
   "id": "e2-35",
   "name": "Auxiliary verbs"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Auxiliary (helping) verbs come before a main verb.\n• Primary auxiliaries: be (am, is, are, was, were) for continuous tenses — She is reading; have (has, have, had) for perfect tenses — They have finished; do (do, does, did) for questions and negatives in simple tenses — Does he play? He did not come.\n• Modal auxiliaries: can, could, may, might, must, shall, should, will, would. They are followed by the bare infinitive: You must report it (not “must to report”).\nThe auxiliary agrees with the subject: he does / they do; she has / we have; I am / he is / they are.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "v": "report"
  },
  "problem": "Make questions: “The police come at night.” / “Ewane has finished.”",
  "steps": [
   "Simple present: use do or does: Do the police come at night?",
   "Present perfect: move has before the subject: Has Ewane finished?"
  ],
  "answer": "Do the police come at night? Has Ewane finished?",
  "checks": []
 },
